# Application Materials Rules (CV / Cover Letter)

## Facts
- NEVER add facts the user did not provide or ask for (e.g., residency status, skills, claims). Polish what exists; do not invent.
- Preserve the exact meaning of findings when simplifying. Example: stockout substitution is "wait for restock rather than buying from other bookstores" — NOT "other regions".
- Keep numbers concrete and unchanged (~62M RMB, ~20%, 1,000+ stores, ratings).

## Style
- Every bullet leads with a strong verb (Showed, Built, Estimated, Lectured).
- One idea per bullet. Do NOT bundle unrelated points with semicolons (finding + reports + benchmark = three bullets).
- Multi-part bullets are allowed only as sequential stages of one activity (build -> estimate -> validate).
- State capabilities directly. No hedges ("PhD-level coursework in X" -> just list X) and no buzzword stuffing ("statistical inference", "data visualization" as standalone skill items).

## HR readability
- Every claim needs its evidence in the same bullet: "Showed through descriptive statistics and regressions that ..."
- Translate econ jargon to plain language: GMM/bootstrap inference -> bootstrap tests; first-best welfare -> best-possible outcome; counterfactual -> what-if.
- Keep keyword-scan terms recruiters look for: Python, R, C++, AWS, HPC/SLURM, 2SLS, LASSO, Monte Carlo, AI-based OCR.
- Skills lines list only concrete tools.

## Consistency
- CV and cover letter must use identical phrasing and numbers for the same fact.
- Graduation date appears once (Education), not repeated in the summary.

## Workflow
- After any .tex edit: run pdflatex twice, then delete .aux/.log/.out.
- CV and cover letter must each stay on 1 page.
- Commit only when the user explicitly asks; verb-first, one-line commit messages; logical groups in separate commits.
